"Namaste! I am Ritu, an STP operator like you. Today we learn on-site water testing.": "audio/en/why/01.mp3",
"Water testing is like a daily health check-up of the plant.": "audio/en/why/02.mp3",
"Every day we check: pH, DO, turbidity, MLSS, TSS, COD and BOD signs, oil and grease, sludge settling, colour and smell.": "audio/en/why/03.mp3",
"Why? To find problems early, and take action before water quality fails.": "audio/en/why/04.mp3",
"Remember five words: Check, Record, Understand, Act, Improve.": "audio/en/why/05.mp3",
"Tap the five steps in the correct order.": "audio/en/why/06.mp3",
"Find problems early. Act before water quality fails.": "audio/en/why/07.mp3",
"Colour is the first sign of water quality. Just look.": "audio/en/colour/01.mp3",
"Inlet sewage may be grey or dark. That is normal.": "audio/en/colour/02.mp3",
"The aeration tank should not be black. Treated water should be clear or light.": "audio/en/colour/03.mp3",
"Black water, very dark brown water, yellow treated water, muddy outlet, or floating sludge and scum: these are problems.": "audio/en/colour/04.mp3",
"Possible reasons: low aeration, septic sewage, poor biological treatment, sludge carryover, or a filter problem.": "audio/en/colour/05.mp3",
"Your actions: check DO in the aeration tank, check the blower, check sludge settling, check filter backwash. If colour stays abnormal, inform your supervisor.": "audio/en/colour/06.mp3",
"Tap every beaker that shows a problem.": "audio/en/colour/07.mp3",
"Treated water must be clear. Black tank means check the air.": "audio/en/colour/08.mp3",
"Smell is a very important field check. Your nose is a free instrument.": "audio/en/smell/01.mp3",
"Inlet sewage may have a mild smell. Treated water should not have a strong bad smell.": "audio/en/smell/02.mp3",
"Rotten egg smell, septic smell, strong chemical smell, or bad smell from the aeration tank or final water: these are warning signs.": "audio/en/smell/03.mp3",
"Reasons: low DO, blower not running, diffuser choking, sewage stagnation, old sludge, or anaerobic condition.": "audio/en/smell/04.mp3",
"Actions: check the DO reading, check the blower and air supply, check equalisation tank mixing, check sludge wasting. If the smell is strong, inform your supervisor immediately.": "audio/en/smell/05.mp3",
"Strong rotten egg smell from the aeration tank. What do you do?": "audio/en/smell/06.mp3",
"Rotten egg smell means low oxygen. Check the blower, inform supervisor.": "audio/en/smell/07.mp3",
"pH tells if water is acidic, neutral, or alkaline. Bacteria work well only in the right pH range.": "audio/en/ph/01.mp3",
"Normal range is 6.5 to 8.5. Below 6.5 is acidic. Above 8.5 is alkaline.": "audio/en/ph/02.mp3",
"How to test: take the sample in a clean container. Rinse the container with the same sample.": "audio/en/ph/03.mp3",
"Dip the pH probe or pH strip. Wait for a stable reading. Note it in the logbook. Clean the probe after testing.": "audio/en/ph/04.mp3",
"If pH is out of range, the problem may be the source water or wastewater discharge. Check the inlet, outlet source, and chemical dosing.": "audio/en/ph/05.mp3",
"Three pH readings. Tap Low, OK, or High for each.": "audio/en/ph/06.mp3",
"pH 6.5 to 8.5 is OK.": "audio/en/ph/07.mp3",
"DO means dissolved oxygen in the water. Bacteria need oxygen to break down pollution.": "audio/en/do/01.mp3",
"Check DO in the aeration tank. Normal range: 1.5 to 3.0 mg/L.": "audio/en/do/02.mp3",
"How to test: take the DO meter. Dip the probe in the aeration tank sample. Wait for a stable reading. Note it. Clean the probe.": "audio/en/do/03.mp3",
"Below 1.0 means low oxygen. This causes bad smell, poor treatment, and black sludge.": "audio/en/do/04.mp3",
"Above 4 means excess aeration is possible.": "audio/en/do/05.mp3",
"Low DO? Check the blower, diffuser, air valve, and leakage. High DO? Check blower timing and inform your supervisor.": "audio/en/do/06.mp3",
"Move the DO slider. Then answer: DO reads 0.8. What is your first check?": "audio/en/do/07.mp3",
"Keep DO between 1.5 and 3.0 mg/L.": "audio/en/do/08.mp3",
"Turbidity means how clear the water is. TSS means solid particles in the water. Both tell us about clarity.": "audio/en/clarity/01.mp3",
"Check at three places: clarifier outlet, filter outlet, and final treated water.": "audio/en/clarity/02.mp3",
"Clarifier outlet may be slightly cloudy. Filter outlet slightly hazy. Final treated water must be clear and clean.": "audio/en/clarity/03.mp3",
"How to check: take the sample in a clean transparent container. Hold it against light. Look for cloudiness and particles. Use a turbidity meter if available. Compare the three samples.": "audio/en/clarity/04.mp3",
"Muddy water, visible particles, or sludge particles in the outlet are abnormal.": "audio/en/clarity/05.mp3",
"Reasons: poor settling, high MLSS, filter choking, backwash not done properly, excess flow, or improper sludge wasting.": "audio/en/clarity/06.mp3",
"Actions: check clarifier settling, check sludge condition, check the filter, do backwash if required, and inform your supervisor.": "audio/en/clarity/07.mp3",
"Which sample must be the clearest?": "audio/en/clarity/08.mp3",
"Clear water means good treatment.": "audio/en/clarity/09.mp3",
"SVI tells us how well the sludge settles in the aeration tank.": "audio/en/svi/01.mp3",
"Take 1 litre of aeration tank sample in a measuring cylinder. Keep it undisturbed for 30 minutes.": "audio/en/svi/02.mp3",
"Watch how the sludge settles. Check the clear water layer on top. Check the sludge volume at the bottom. Record it.": "audio/en/svi/03.mp3",
"Very low sludge means low biomass: inform your supervisor. Very high sludge means excess sludge: check sludge wasting.": "audio/en/svi/04.mp3",
"Sludge not settling means bulking or a process issue: check pH, DO, and sludge return. Black sludge means septic condition: check aeration. Floating sludge means old sludge or gas: check wasting and DO.": "audio/en/svi/05.mp3",
"Run the settling test. Then tap the cylinder that shows a septic condition.": "audio/en/svi/06.mp3",
"1 litre, 30 minutes, record the sludge volume.": "audio/en/svi/07.mp3",
"MLSS means mixed liquor suspended solids. In simple words: how much active bacteria sludge is in the aeration tank.": "audio/en/mlss/01.mp3",
"Low MLSS means fewer bacteria and weak treatment.": "audio/en/mlss/02.mp3",
"High MLSS means excess sludge, poor settling, sludge carryover, and higher oxygen demand.": "audio/en/mlss/03.mp3",
"Watch for: very low or very high sludge, sludge not settling, black sludge, floating sludge, high TSS in the outlet, and foaming in the aeration tank.": "audio/en/mlss/04.mp3",
"Most actions are the same: check DO, pH, sludge wasting, aeration, blower and diffuser, clarifier and return sludge. And always inform your supervisor.": "audio/en/mlss/05.mp3",
"Match what you see with what you check.": "audio/en/mlss/06.mp3",
"Low MLSS: weak treatment. High MLSS: poor settling.": "audio/en/mlss/07.mp3",
"COD and BOD show the organic pollution load in wastewater. On-site, we watch for the signs.": "audio/en/codbod/01.mp3",
"Observe five things: inlet colour, smell, foam, the aeration tank, and settling.": "audio/en/codbod/02.mp3",
"Warning signs: strong bad smell, dark inlet, low DO, high foam, poor settling, septic condition, high turbidity, or a sudden change in water quality.": "audio/en/codbod/03.mp3",
"Actions: check inlet condition, check DO, check the aeration tank, check sludge settling. Inform your supervisor if a sudden load is observed.": "audio/en/codbod/04.mp3",
"Which of these are warning signs of high organic load?": "audio/en/codbod/05.mp3",
"Sudden change in smell, colour, foam, DO or settling means high load.": "audio/en/codbod/06.mp3",
"Oil and grease come mainly from kitchens, canteens, hotels, and commercial wastewater.": "audio/en/oil/01.mp3",
"Look for: floating oil layers, grease deposits, sticky scum, bad smell, and slippery surfaces.": "audio/en/oil/02.mp3",
"Reasons: the oil and grease trap is not cleaned, or kitchen wastewater is entering the plant directly.": "audio/en/oil/03.mp3",
"Actions: check the oil and grease trap, remove floating grease, clean the trap as per schedule. Inform your supervisor if a heavy load is coming.": "audio/en/oil/04.mp3",
"Look at the tank. Tap where you see oil.": "audio/en/oil/05.mp3",
"Clean the trap on schedule. Stop kitchen water entering directly.": "audio/en/oil/06.mp3",
"TSS test in the lab. Items: sample bottle, measuring cylinder, filter paper, weighing balance, filtration setup, oven, desiccator.": "audio/en/tsslab/01.mp3",
"Take treated water in a clean bottle. Measure a fixed volume: less for dirty water, more for clear water.": "audio/en/tsslab/02.mp3",
"Dry the filter paper and weigh it. This is W1.": "audio/en/tsslab/03.mp3",
"Pass the sample through the filter paper. Solids stay on the paper.": "audio/en/tsslab/04.mp3",
"Dry the paper in an oven at 103 to 105 °C. Cool it in a desiccator. Weigh again. This is W2.": "audio/en/tsslab/05.mp3",
"TSS in mg/L equals W2 minus W1, times 1000, divided by sample volume in litres.": "audio/en/tsslab/06.mp3",
"Calculate TSS with these lab numbers.": "audio/en/tsslab/07.mp3",
"TSS = (W2 − W1) × 1000 ÷ litres.": "audio/en/tsslab/08.mp3",
"MLSS test is the same method, but with a well-mixed sample from the aeration tank.": "audio/en/mlsslab/01.mp3",
"Measure 50, 100, or 250 millilitres, depending on sludge concentration.": "audio/en/mlsslab/02.mp3",
"Weigh dry filter paper: W1. Filter the sample. Dry at 103 to 105 °C. Cool in desiccator. Weigh again: W2.": "audio/en/mlsslab/03.mp3",
"MLSS in mg/L equals W2 minus W1, times 1000, divided by sample volume in litres.": "audio/en/mlsslab/04.mp3",
"Tap the MLSS test steps in the correct order.": "audio/en/mlsslab/05.mp3",
"Same formula as TSS. Sample from the aeration tank.": "audio/en/mlsslab/06.mp3",
"COD means chemical oxygen demand: the oxygen needed to chemically oxidise organic matter. This is a lab test.": "audio/en/codlab/01.mp3",
"Take a well-mixed sample. Measure 50 millilitres into a COD digestion flask.": "audio/en/codlab/02.mp3",
"Add 1.5 grams of potassium dichromate reagent. Add 20 millilitres of sulfuric acid reagent slowly. It becomes hot. Add 2 to 3 boiling chips.": "audio/en/codlab/03.mp3",
"Attach the reflux condenser. Heat and reflux for 2 hours with gentle boiling. Then cool to room temperature.": "audio/en/codlab/04.mp3",
"Dilute with distilled water to about 150 millilitres. Titrate with ferrous ammonium sulfate using 2 to 3 drops of ferroin indicator.": "audio/en/codlab/05.mp3",
"End point: colour changes from green to reddish brown. COD equals A minus B, times N, times 8000, divided by sample volume in millilitres.": "audio/en/codlab/06.mp3",
"Safety first: wear gloves, goggles, and apron. Add acid slowly. Work in a well-ventilated area. Keep glassware clean.": "audio/en/codlab/07.mp3",
"Before starting the COD test, tap everything you must do.": "audio/en/codlab/08.mp3",
"Acid slowly. Reflux 2 hours. Green to reddish brown.": "audio/en/codlab/09.mp3",
"BOD5 means biochemical oxygen demand for 5 days: the oxygen microorganisms use to break down organic matter in 5 days at 20 °C.": "audio/en/bodlab/01.mp3",
"Take a well-mixed sample. Prepare dilutions if required. Take two DO bottles: a sample bottle and a blank bottle with dilution water.": "audio/en/bodlab/02.mp3",
"Fill both bottles completely with no air bubbles. Overflow 2 to 3 times and seal. Measure DO immediately in both bottles. Record as DO1. Incubate both in the dark at 20 °C for 5 days.": "audio/en/bodlab/03.mp3",
"After 5 days, measure DO again. Record as DO2. BOD5 equals DO1 minus DO2, times the dilution factor.": "audio/en/bodlab/04.mp3",
"Quality check: the blank bottle DO must not change by more than 0.2. Choose dilution so DO after 5 days stays above 1. Clean bottles, caps and probe after testing.": "audio/en/bodlab/05.mp3",
"Calculate BOD5 with these readings.": "audio/en/bodlab/06.mp3",
"No air bubbles. 5 days, 20 °C, in the dark.": "audio/en/bodlab/07.mp3",
